# Wiring an endpoint

The chain is one-way and every link is required:

```
FEGuides/<Module>.md   (contract, in the backend repo)
   ↓
src/dtos/req/<domain>-request.dto.ts   ·   src/dtos/res/<domain>-response.dto.ts
   ↓
src/services/<domain>.service.ts        axios; the ONLY layer that touches raw shapes
   ↓
src/services/mappers/<domain>.mapper.ts DTO → UI domain type
   ↓
src/hooks/use-<domain>-queries.ts  ·  use-<domain>-mutations.ts
   ↓
src/components/<module>/<Thing>Content.tsx
```

Copy the nearest existing module end to end — CAPA and Incidents are the most complete. That is
faster and more correct than assembling from this description.

## 1. Read the contract first

The API lives in `connect-codeswift/Neptune-Ehss-BE`; contracts live in its `FEGuides/` directory, one
markdown file per module. Read the entry for your endpoint: route, method, required permission,
request body, the exact `dataModel` shape, and the error cases.

`sw.json` at this repo's root is a Swagger dump and is **stale** — a rough reference at best.
Reads are anonymous projections server-side, so backend entity names are not a reliable guide
either. If there is no FEGuide entry, the contract does not exist — ask the backend.

## 2. DTOs

- `src/dtos/req/<domain>-request.dto.ts` — request bodies.
- `src/dtos/res/<domain>-response.dto.ts` — response shapes.
- `src/dtos/res/api-envelope.dto.ts` already defines `ApiEnvelopeDto<T>`, the wrapper every
  endpoint uses. Don't redeclare it.

Comment each exported type with the verb and path it belongs to:
`/** PUT /api/Document/DocApproval (JSON body). */`

Model what the API actually sends, casing quirks included — normalizing happens in the service.

## 3. Service — the only layer allowed to touch `unknown`

`src/services/<domain>.service.ts`. One exported `async function` per endpoint, using the shared
axios instance (`http`, the default export of `@/lib/axios`):

```ts
/** POST /api/Incident/incident */
export async function createIncident(body: CreateIncidentRequestDto) {
  const { data } = await http.post<ApiEnvelopeDto<IncidentResponseDto>>(
    "/Incident/incident",
    body,
  );
  return data.dataModel;
}
```

The backend mixes camelCase and PascalCase, sometimes inside one payload. Normalize it here with
the small coercion helpers (`isRecord` / `readProp` / `asNumber` / `asString` / `asBoolean`)
declared at the top of each service file — see `document.service.ts`. **They are duplicated per
file on purpose.** Don't refactor them into a shared util without being asked.

No React, no state, no toasts in this file.

## 4. Mapper

`src/services/mappers/<domain>.mapper.ts` — DTO → the UI domain type, which lives in
`src/components/<module>/<module>-types.ts`. If you catch yourself writing
`item.Status ?? item.status` in a component, the service or mapper is missing a step.

## 5. Hook

`src/hooks/use-<domain>-queries.ts` for reads, `use-<domain>-mutations.ts` for writes. Both
`"use client"`. Export a **query-key factory object**, not loose consts:

```ts
export const incidentQueryKeys = {
  all: ["incidents"] as const,
  list: (params: IncidentListParams) => ["incidents", "list", params] as const,
  detail: (id: number) => ["incidents", "detail", id] as const,
};

export function useIncidentList(params: IncidentListParams, enabled: boolean) {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: incidentQueryKeys.list(params),
    queryFn: () => getIncidents(params),
    select: mapIncidentList,
    enabled,
  });
}
```

- Apply the mapper through `select`, not inside `queryFn`.
- Thread `enabled` explicitly from the caller. **Never default it to `true` for anything
  requiring auth** — that fires a 401 on every logged-out render.
- Mutations invalidate `<domain>QueryKeys.all` in `onSuccess` and surface errors through
  `src/lib/toast.ts`.

## 6. Content component

`src/components/<module>/<Thing>Content.tsx` — a client component that owns the fetching for one
route, with the page file staying thin. ~36 of these exist; copy the nearest.

It should never see `dataModel`, an axios instance, or a raw API field name. Use
`src/components/ui/` primitives and `Text.tsx` for typography. Props typed `Readonly<XProps>`
(Sonar S6759).

## 7. Gate on permission, and on the module

- If the endpoint carries a `[HasPermission("Module.Action")]` (the FEGuide states it), gate the
  UI with `src/lib/jwt-permissions.ts` so a button that would 403 does not render. This is UX,
  not security — the backend enforces role and permission independently.
- A new dashboard route also needs its entry in `src/lib/ehs-modules.ts` / `src/lib/app-nav.ts`,
  or the sidebar will not show it.

## 8. Files and dates

Uploads go to Cloudinary client-side first (`src/lib/upload-to-cloudinary.ts`); only the URL is
sent to the API — that is what every `pdfPath` / `filePath` holds. Every datetime on the wire is
ISO-8601 **with an explicit offset**; never build a naive string.

## 9. Replacing placeholder data

Before extending any `dummy-*` / `*.dummy.ts` file, check whether a real service and hook already
exist for that screen. When you wire the real endpoint, delete the placeholder rather than
leaving both paths alive.

## 10. Verify

```bash
npm run lint
npm run build     # the real typecheck — there are no tests
```
